CAPSTONE (tshark app port): wmem's block sizes for the sublet heap arm. With
-DCAPSTONE_WMEM_BLOCK_BYTES=<n>, the BLOCK and BLOCK_FAST allocators take their blocks in n-byte
pieces instead of 8 MiB and 2 MiB. The sublet heap is a buddy allocator over a 16 MiB pool, which
cannot hold three 8 MiB blocks and a 2 MiB one; the blocks are mostly empty (about 1.5 MB is
filled at tshark's peak, the plan's M0 item 4). Only host/build-domain.sh's sublet arm defines
the macro, when it compiles these two files itself; every other build is unchanged.

--- a/wsutil/wmem/wmem_allocator_block.c	2026-09-25 08:22:41.161178967 +0800
+++ b/wsutil/wmem/wmem_allocator_block.c	2026-09-25 08:22:41.177178967 +0800
@@ -139,7 +139,15 @@
  * 8MB is a pretty arbitrary value - it's big enough that it should last a while
  * and small enough that a mostly-unused one doesn't waste *too* much. It's
  * also a nice power of two, of course. */
+#ifdef CAPSTONE_WMEM_BLOCK_BYTES
+/* capstone64, the sublet heap arm only (the tshark port's host/build-domain.sh, TSAPP_HEAP=sublet):
+ * the domain's heap is a buddy allocator over a 16 MiB pool, and three 8 MiB blocks plus a 2 MiB
+ * one do not fit in it. A block of exactly a power of two fills one buddy block, since that heap
+ * keeps no header in band. Every other build leaves the macro undefined. */
+#define WMEM_BLOCK_SIZE (CAPSTONE_WMEM_BLOCK_BYTES)
+#else
 #define WMEM_BLOCK_SIZE (8 * 1024 * 1024)
+#endif
 
 /* The header for an entire OS-level 'block' of memory */
 typedef struct _wmem_block_hdr_t {
